i=items[Q];i['statement']='Assume the Axiom of Choice for the sheafification and represented-sheaf suppliers used below. Work on a fixed big fppf site of $k$-schemes as in Stacks §34.7. '+i['statement'];i['statement']=i['statement'].replace('a finite family of flat morphisms locally of finite presentation whose disjoint union is surjective onto $T$','a set-indexed family of flat morphisms locally of finite presentation which is jointly surjective onto $T$').replace('The quotient sheaf is generally strictly bigger than the naive quotient presheaf;','The natural map from the naive quotient presheaf to its quotient sheaf is generally not an isomorphism; sheafification can both identify sections that agree locally and introduce sections that only have local representatives.');i['strategy']+=r''' Covering families need not be finite globally. Over each affine target open, openness of the covering maps and quasi-compactness allow a finite affine refinement, exactly as in the proof of [[lem-nonaffine-fppf-descent-of-scheme-morphisms]]. Sheafification is the two-step plus construction of Stacks Sites and Sheaves §7.10, Theorem 7.10.10, Definition 7.10.11 and Proposition 7.10.12. The naive presheaf definition itself uses no selection, but the published represented-sheaf supplier explicitly assumes AC.''';deps(Q,'def-axiom-of-choice')
i=items[D];i['statement']=i['statement'].replace('its image, with the reduced subscheme structure, is the **orbit subscheme** $O_x$ ([[def-scheme-theoretic-image]], [[def-locally-closed-immersion]]).',r'''For separated finite-type $X$, its underlying image is the **orbit set**. The **reduced orbit subscheme** $O_x$ means this locally closed image with reduced structure, when local closedness is established; the orbit lemma below constructs it for smooth $G$. For a nonsmooth group, the orbit map need not factor through this reduced subscheme: translation of $\alpha_p$ on $\mathbb A^1$ at $0$ has a one-point reduced orbit but a nonconstant infinitesimal orbit map. A factorization $G\to O_x$ must therefore be justified or explicitly assumed.''').replace('it is a closed subscheme of $G$, and','for separated finite-type $X$ it is a closed subscheme of $G$, and');i['strategy']+=r''' Do not identify the image with the scheme-theoretic image: the latter is closed and is normally the orbit closure, whereas the orbit is only locally closed. Fibers and the kernel pair of $\varrho_x:G\to X$ are always defined; a kernel pair over $O_x$ requires a factorization through the reduced orbit.'''
edit(A,s=r'''Let $G$ be any finite-type $k$-group scheme acting on a separated finite-type $k$-scheme $X$, and let $x\in X(k)$. (a) The scheme-theoretic stabilizer $H=G_x=G\times_X\operatorname{Spec}k$ is a closed subgroup scheme, with $H(R)=\{g:gx_R=x_R\}$ for every $k$-algebra $R$. (b) The scheme fiber $F_y=G\times_X\operatorname{Spec}k$ over $y\in X(k)$ is empty unless $y$ belongs to the underlying image of the orbit map. If $y=g_0x$ with $g_0\in G(k)$, then $F_y=g_0H$. In general, if $F_y$ is nonempty, it becomes such a translate after a finite field extension carrying a point of the fiber; it is an fppf right $H$-torsor, and need not have a $k$-point. For $g\in G(k)$, $G_{gx}=gHg^{-1}$. (c) The fiber over $(y,x)$ of $(g,z)\mapsto(gz,z):G\times X\to X\times X$ is canonically $F_y$. (d) The morphism $G\times H\to G\times_XG$, $(g,h)\mapsto(g,gh)$, is an isomorphism. If $\varrho_x$ factors through a locally closed orbit subscheme $O_x\hookrightarrow X$, this is also the kernel pair over $O_x$, because an immersion is a monomorphism.''',t=r'''The rational point of separated $X$ is a closed immersion (a section of a separated morphism), so its pullback gives the closed stabilizer. The action axioms show its point functor is closed under identity, multiplication and inverse, and [[lem-closed-subgroup-scheme-valued-point-criterion]] supplies the subgroup scheme conclusion. Translation by $g_0$ gives the fiber and conjugacy identities when $g_0$ exists. A nonempty finite-type fiber has a closed point whose residue field is a finite extension of $k$ by [[cor-field-finite-type-over-a-field-is-a-finite-extension]]; over that extension translation trivializes it as $H$. This extension is an fppf cover, so the fiber is an fppf torsor, without claiming a rational translate. For (c), fiber points force $z=x$ and $gx=y$. For (d), on every test algebra equal images $gx=g'x$ are equivalent to the unique relation $g^{-1}g'\in H(R)$; the inverse morphism is $(g,g')\mapsto(g,g^{-1}g')$. These explicit natural formulas prove a scheme isomorphism, including infinitesimal points. Finally equality in an immersion target is the same as equality in $X$, giving the kernel-pair assertion when the factorization exists.''');deps(A,'cor-field-finite-type-over-a-field-is-a-finite-extension')
edit(C,t=r'''Apply Stacks Lemma 39.20.3, but spell out its injectivity step. A section of the quotient sheaf is locally represented by a section of $U$, by the plus-construction description of sheafification. Thus (2) gives local lifts of every $M$-section and makes $U/R\to h_M$ surjective. If two quotient sections have the same image, refine a covering until both are represented by $u,v\in U(T_i)$. Their common $M$-image defines a section of $U\times_MU$; (3) lifts it locally to $R$, making those local representatives equivalent. They therefore agree as quotient-sheaf sections. Sheaf separatedness glues this equality, proving injectivity. Condition (1) gives the factorization, and represented functors are fppf sheaves under the explicitly assumed AC through [[lem-nonaffine-fppf-descent-of-scheme-morphisms]]. No false assertion that related points of $U$ are equal in $U$ is used.''')
edit(P,s=r'''Assume the Axiom of Choice, inherited from [[thm-projective-bundle-represents-line-quotients]]. Let $G$ be an affine finite-type $k$-group scheme, let $V$ be finite-dimensional, and let $r:G\to\operatorname{GL}_V$ be the rational representation of [[def-rational-representation-and-comodule-of-an-affine-group-scheme]]. In the repository's quotient convention, $\mathbb P(V)$ represents invertible quotients of $V_T$ and has the natural action $g\cdot[q]=[q\circ r(g)^{-1}]$. Define the space of lines by $\mathbf P_{\mathrm{lines}}(V):=\mathbb P(V^\vee)$, using the dual representation $r^\vee(g)=(r(g)^{-1})^\vee$. Its $T$-points are equivalently rank-one locally direct summand subbundles $L\subseteq V_T$, with action $L\mapsto r(g)L$. A line $L\subseteq V$ gives the point $[L]$ via the rank-one quotient $V^\vee\twoheadrightarrow L^\vee$, not via $V\twoheadrightarrow V/L$. The scheme stabilizer of this point has $R$-points exactly $\{g:r(g)L_R=L_R\}$. Therefore any closed subgroup scheme with that line-stabilizer functor is $G_{[L]}$. No smoothness of $G$ or its subgroup is needed.''',t=r'''Use the exact rank-one quotient representability theorem on $V$ and $V^\vee$, with its stated AC. A quotient $V_T^\vee\to M$ dualizes to the locally split rank-one injection $M^\vee\to V_T$; conversely a line subbundle dualizes to a surjection, because its locally free quotient makes the sequence locally split. Duality gives the natural inverse bijections. For quotient points, $g\cdot(h\cdot q)=q r(h)^{-1}r(g)^{-1}=q r(gh)^{-1}$; inversion is anti-multiplicative, not multiplicative. On the dual representation this is exactly the action on line subbundles $L\mapsto r(g)L$. Naturality on every test scheme and Yoneda yield the action morphisms and the action axioms. The restriction quotient $V^\vee\to L^\vee$ is preserved as an isomorphism class exactly when its annihilator, and hence the double-annihilator line $L_T$, is preserved. This computes the scheme stabilizer on every algebra, rather than only over fields.''');deps(P,'def-axiom-of-choice')
edit(O,t=r'''Follow Milne 1.65 and 7.4, filling the scheme and field-change steps. Form the scheme-theoretic closure $Y$ of $\varrho_x:G\to X$ using [[thm-scheme-theoretic-image-quasi-compact-morphism]]. On an affine target chart the ideal is $\ker(A\to\prod_jB_j)$ for a finite affine cover of its inverse image, as in that supplier's proof. For every field extension $K/k$, flat tensor extension preserves this kernel and finite product, so $Y_K$ is the scheme-theoretic closure of $G_K\to X_K$. The $B_j\otimes K$ are reduced because $G$ is smooth; thus $A_K/I_K$ injects into a product of reduced rings, and $Y$ is geometrically reduced. Over an algebraic closure $K$, apply classical Chevalley constructibility to the reduced finite-type source and target charts. For finitely many components, remove their intersections and choose a dense open in the image on each component; the resulting nonempty open of the reduced closure lies in the image. Closed-point and scheme topologies agree on closed/open/constructible subsets of finite-type schemes by the Nullstellensatz dictionary (Milne A.22). Translates of this open cover every closed orbit point, so the image is open in $Y_K$. The orbit set is saturated under $|Y_K|\to|Y|$: nonempty fibers remain nonempty after field extension, since tensor products of field extensions over a common field are nonzero. This projection is surjective and closed: $K/k$ is algebraic, so it is an integral map on affine charts, and integral maps are closed by lying-over and going-up; a surjective closed map is a quotient map. Hence the image is open in $Y$, defining $O_x$ as this open reduced subscheme. The orbit map factors through it and formation of $O_x$ commutes with this algebraic-closure base change. The action preserves $O_x$ scheme-theoretically: after that field change, the product $G_K\times O_{x,K}$ is reduced and its closed points map into $O_{x,K}$; functions defining its closure pull back to zero, and the open-complement preimage is empty. Flat descent of vanishing gives the same factorization over $k$. For flatness over $K$, remove intersections of the finitely many irreducible components of $O_{x,K}$, apply [[thm-generic-flatness-morphisms]] separately on these Noetherian integral charts, and combine the resulting dense open subsets. Translate this open by $G(K)$ to cover every closed point of the homogeneous target; source translation and target action identify the orbit maps over these opens, so they are flat. The translates cover the whole finite-type scheme (a nonempty closed complement would have a closed point), making the orbit map flat and surjective over $K$. On affine charts [[thm-faithfully-flat-descent-of-flatness]] descends flatness to $k$, and surjectivity is already known. The morphism is locally of finite presentation because both schemes have finite type over a field and the rings are Noetherian. Finally $O_{x,K}$ is reduced and has a nonempty regular locus by [[thm-nonempty-regular-locus-reduced-variety-perfect-field]]; regularity equals smoothness over the perfect field $K$ by [[thm-regular-equals-smooth-over-perfect-field]]. The smooth locus is open and invariant under the transitive action, so it is all of $O_{x,K}$. Geometric regularity descends under field extension by [[lem-ag-geometric-regularity-field-tests]], proving smoothness of $O_x/k$. This argument needs neither connectedness of $G$ nor smoothness of its scheme stabilizer.''')
deps(O,'thm-scheme-theoretic-image-quasi-compact-morphism','thm-nonempty-regular-locus-reduced-variety-perfect-field','thm-regular-equals-smooth-over-perfect-field','thm-smooth-locus-open','lem-ag-geometric-regularity-field-tests','thm-affine-nullstellensatz-correspondence','thm-finite-generation-and-finite-presentation-over-a-noetherian-ring','thm-integral-ring-map-spectrum-is-closed')
