i=items[DIM];i['statement']=i['statement'].replace('hence has dimension $\\dim G_x$ in the sense of [[def-dimension-classical-variety]]','hence has underlying topological dimension $\\dim G_x$, equivalently the dimension of its reduction; $G_x$ may be nonreduced ([[def-dimension-noetherian-topological-space]])');i['strategy']=r'''By [[lem-nonaffine-connected-group-geometrically-connected]], the smooth connected group $G$ is integral over the algebraically closed field, so its orbit and closure are irreducible. The orbit lemma makes $O_x$ a smooth locally closed classical variety. A nonempty fiber over a closed $k$-point has a $k$-point by the weak Nullstellensatz, so [[lem-action-map-fibres-and-stabilizer-subscheme]] gives an actual scheme translate of $G_x$; take reductions when applying classical dimension suppliers. Apply [[thm-generic-fibre-dimension]] to the dominant map between the irreducible classical varieties $G\to O_x$: on the supplied nonempty open its reduced fibers have dimension $\dim G-\dim O_x$, while all these fibers are translates of the same reduced stabilizer. Thus $\dim G=\dim G_x+\dim O_x$ even when $G_x$ is nonsmooth. The orbit is open in its irreducible closure. Its boundary is a proper closed subset, invariant under all $G(k)$ automorphisms; every boundary orbit has closure in that boundary and strictly smaller dimension by [[lem-dimension-nonempty-open-subset]]. This proves the strict-dimension closure statement. Repeatedly pass to a boundary orbit if the orbit is not closed; dimensions strictly decrease in the nonnegative integers, so the process terminates at a closed orbit. No infinite choice is required. The AC hypothesis is inherited from the exact geometric, dimension and Nullstellensatz suppliers.''';deps(DIM,'lem-nonaffine-connected-group-geometrically-connected','lem-dimension-nonempty-open-subset','cor-weak-nullstellensatz-algebraically-closed-coordinate-form')
i=items[H];i['statement']=i['statement'].replace('the induced action on $P(V)$','the induced action on $\\mathbf P_{\\mathrm{lines}}(V)=\\mathbb P(V^\\vee)$').replace('$G/H\\to P(V)$','$G/H\\to\\mathbf P_{\\mathrm{lines}}(V)$');i['strategy']=i['strategy'].replace('on $P(V)$','on $\\mathbf P_{\\mathrm{lines}}(V)=\\mathbb P(V^\\vee)$').replace('$O_x\\subseteq P(V)$','$O_x\\subseteq\\mathbf P_{\\mathrm{lines}}(V)$').replace('scheme $P(V)$','scheme $\\mathbf P_{\\mathrm{lines}}(V)$');i['strategy']+=r''' The subgroup supplier supplies a line in $V$, so its point is the restriction quotient $V^\vee\to L^\vee$; this explicitly reconciles Milne's line convention with the repository's quotient projective bundle convention. Every finite-type group and the representation used here satisfy the affine hypotheses of the representation definition.'''
i=items[E];i['statement']='Assume the Axiom of Choice inherited from the homogeneous-space and projective-bundle suppliers. '+i['statement'];i['strategy']=i['strategy'].replace('The standard representation is faithful, so the induced action on $\\mathbb P^1_k$ is the usual action on lines in $k^2$;',r'In dimension two, the quotient projective space $\mathbb P(V)$ identifies equivariantly with lines in $V$ by taking the kernel of a rank-one quotient; hence the induced action is the usual action on lines.');i['strategy']=i['strategy'].replace('Hence $O_o$ is the complement of the diagonal;',r'The pointwise calculation identifies the geometric orbit set with the complement of the diagonal. To identify the scheme and the quotient over every base, a pair of line subbundles in this complement has invertible wedge determinant, so their direct sum is $V_T$. On a Zariski cover choose frames for both line bundles; the two frame columns give an invertible matrix. These lifts prove that $G\to Y\setminus\Delta$ is locally a product with $T=\mathbf G_m^2$, hence is faithfully flat and locally of finite presentation, with the stated kernel pair. The representability criterion therefore gives $G/T\cong Y\setminus\Delta$ as schemes, not just on field-valued points;');deps(E,'def-axiom-of-choice',C)
